\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\qc\fs32\b Who Owns Academic Knowledge?\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\qc\fs30\b The Evolution of Intellectual Property Rights in American Universities\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\qc\fs24 Anonymous research manuscript\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\qc\fs24 10 October 2026\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\qc\fs24 Historical policy evidence, legal distinctions, and limits of identification\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\qc\fs24 Approximate total word count: 6,500, including tables and references\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Research status: the archive and lexical analyses are reproducible. Selected provisions have been checked against accessible source context using AI-assisted close reading. Independent legal annotation and comprehensive historical source authentication remain incomplete. This manuscript is not designated submission-ready.\par

\page
\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b Abstract\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 University intellectual property policies define knowledge and allocate rights through different provisions. Treating broader definitions as broader ownership can therefore misidentify institutional change. We audit 480 observed policy vintages from 150 American universities, 1944\u8211?2025, distinguishing them from carried-forward panel years. The original screen reproduces 2,779 candidate sentences in 449 records. Across 330 within-university comparisons, research-data terminology appears in 57 previously unmentioned cases and disappears in 24; these are lexical transitions, not verified transfers of rights. Contextual readings reveal conditional claims, academic-work exceptions, reciprocal licenses, and assignment language that sentence-level screening misses. Historical reprints and campus-specific documents complicate comparisons further. The evidence establishes heterogeneous textual arrangements and the inadequacy of vocabulary alone as an ownership measure. It does not identify the prevalence or direction of legal expansion. We provide a reproducible archive audit, a multidimensional coding protocol, and a source-linked record of the remaining identification problems.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Keywords: university intellectual property; ownership; assignment; institutional change; academic knowledge; legal text.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 1 Introduction\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 A university can add software to its definition of intellectual property while continuing to leave educational software with its creator. It can also replace a promise to assign an invention with language purporting to make the assignment immediately, without adding any new category of knowledge. The first change enlarges a vocabulary; the second changes the stated mechanism by which rights are transferred. Neither can be understood from a count of asset names alone. The distinction matters because university policies help organize decisions about research, publication, licensing and collaboration, yet the document that describes those decisions is not itself conclusive evidence of legal title.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 This article asks how American universities have described the boundaries of ownership over academic knowledge and what evidence is needed to establish that those boundaries changed. Its empirical starting point is an existing historical policy corpus covering 150 universities. Rather than presume an expansion of institutional control, we separate the objects named in a policy from the actors, operations and conditions through which rights are allocated. A claim to an invention made within employment, a nonexclusive license to a course resource, and a requirement to disclose a discovery describe different relationships. They should not be collapsed into an index of institutional ownership.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The archive permits a reproducible examination of language across directly observed policy vintages. It does not yet permit a validated national history of changes in legal ownership. That is an empirical conclusion about the available evidence, not an assumption that policy has remained stable. We reproduce the established population, diagnose its documentary structure, and compare adjacent observed vintages within universities. We then use accessible original policies and a historical survey to examine why a lexical transition may or may not correspond to a change in rights. The statistical results are deliberately restricted to quantities that the underlying data identify.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 There is substantial prior work on ownership regimes. Comparative studies distinguish institutional and inventor ownership, and some already separate ownership from control and revenue rights (Kenney and Patton, 2011; Halilem et al., 2017). Policy content has also been coded directly: Jamali (2024) examines staff, student and other ownership arrangements across Australian public universities. We do not claim to introduce the study of university IP rules or the distinction among different rights. The intended contribution is a historical American analysis that follows the chain from a dated source to a specific legal relationship, and from that relationship to a comparable earlier rule. The present evidence supports the archive audit and selected textual comparisons; completing the broader contribution requires contextual validation across the historical sample.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The question is also distinct from research on how clearly policies communicate governance arrangements and from research relating policy communication to commercialization outcomes. We use the original policy sentences and institution identifiers, but do not reconstruct a communication index or estimate licensing and patent-output regressions. The object of interest is the allocation of rights. An institution can communicate a narrow claim clearly, or express a broad claim ambiguously. Those are separate dimensions of institutional organization.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 2 Property rights, scientific work and institutional rules\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Ownership matters in incomplete-contracting accounts because a contract cannot specify every future use of an asset. Residual authority affects the parties' incentives and their positions when unforeseen circumstances arise (Grossman and Hart, 1986; Hart and Moore, 1990; Hart, 1995). Applied to academic knowledge, this perspective suggests asking who may authorize a license, refuse a transfer, release an invention, or continue using a work after title is assigned. It does not imply that the broadest university claim is efficient. The relevant investments include research effort, disclosure, development and access by other researchers; different allocations may favor different investments.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Knowledge also differs from the physical assets that motivate many property-rights models. Information can be used by multiple researchers, legal protection may attach to only part of a research output, and publication can coexist with restrictions on materials or commercial exploitation. The economics of open science emphasizes priority, disclosure and reputation as organizing institutions (Dasgupta and David, 1994). Academic freedom can have value precisely because researchers retain discretion over lines of inquiry that a commercial principal would not immediately pursue (Aghion et al., 2008). A policy that reserves educational use to a creator while assigning commercialization authority to the university therefore creates a divided arrangement, not an all-or-nothing transfer of control.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Institutional rules mediate these choices, but written rules are only one part of institutional change (North, 1990). Administrators may formalize an existing practice, respond to a funding condition, consolidate several policies, or alter the people to whom a rule applies. A longer definition could resolve uncertainty without changing an operative allocation. Conversely, deleting a student exception could alter the scope of a claim even if every category name remains unchanged. These possibilities motivate observable distinctions rather than a single formal prediction of expansion.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The historical literature cautions against treating 1980 as the origin of university patent governance. Palmer (1962) collected university patent policies well before Bayh\u8211?Dole. Studies of twentieth-century patenting locate the legislation within earlier administrative developments and changing research opportunities (Mowery et al., 2001; Sampat, 2006). Organizational accounts likewise trace institution-building and the normalization of technology-transfer practices before the statute (Popp Berman, 2008; Colyvas, 2007). A before-and-after difference at 1980 would confound those developments with legal change, archival availability and changes in research composition. We do not use Bayh\u8211?Dole as a natural experiment.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Normative arguments are contested. Kenney and Patton (2009) criticize the institutional invention-ownership model, while the National Research Council frames technology transfer as an institutional responsibility that should serve broader public purposes (National Research Council, 2011). Comparative European evidence shows that ownership reforms occur alongside other organizational changes (Geuna and Rossi, 2011); the Italian experience similarly links policy development and university patenting without establishing that the same mechanism applies to American institutions (Baldini et al., 2006). These sources support competing interpretations of institutional claims, not a presumption that more claims are either beneficial or harmful.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The incentive literature also gives reason to distinguish allocation from outcomes. Lach and Schankerman (2008) report a relationship between royalty incentives and university invention, whereas Ouellette and Tutt (2020) identify coding problems and challenge the robustness of the earlier evidence. Even a reliable description of royalty shares would not establish who initially owns a work. Nor would an association between a policy and patents identify the effect of its assignment clause. Our choice to analyze rights directly avoids using commercialization as a substitute for legal measurement.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The scientific-commons literature identifies another set of competing mechanisms. Fragmented exclusion rights may obstruct subsequent research (Heller and Eisenberg, 1998), and policy scholarship questions whether privatization always advances publicly funded research (Eisenberg, 1996; Rai and Eisenberg, 2003; Nelson, 2004). Yet a patent can sometimes facilitate the search for a developer (Hellmann, 2007). Empirical studies distinguish citation effects, material-transfer barriers and changes in research access (Murray and Stern, 2007; Walsh et al., 2005; Murray et al., 2016). Those outcomes are not interchangeable. A license to use a material, title to a patent, and authority over a database can affect scientific activity through different channels. Scientific norms may further qualify how formal rights are exercised (Rai, 1999).\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 For data, the distinction is especially consequential. Research-data sharing involves heterogeneous objects, responsibilities and incentives (Borgman, 2012). Contractual arrangements can support a research commons even where proprietary claims are available (Reichman and Uhlir, 2003). FAIR principles address the management and reuse of data; they do not by themselves allocate ownership (Wilkinson et al., 2016). An increase in references to databases could therefore reflect stewardship, disclosure requirements, licensing, or ownership. Only the surrounding rule establishes which interpretation is supported.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 3 What a policy can establish legally\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 A university policy is an institutional statement of a claimed rule. Its legal effect depends on the underlying right and on matters that a text corpus usually does not observe: applicable law, the employment or student relationship, assent, executed agreements, competing assignments, and the facts of creation. We separate an institutional ownership presumption from an assignment obligation and from wording that purports to make a present assignment. We also separate each of these from a judicial determination of title.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The distinction is visible in patent law. The Bayh\u8211?Dole framework addresses rights in inventions made with federal assistance, including a contractor's election to retain title to qualifying subject inventions (United States Congress, 2024). In Stanford v. Roche, the Supreme Court held that the Act does not automatically vest title in federal contractors or authorize them unilaterally to take an inventor's rights (Supreme Court of the United States, 2011, pp. 785\u8211?789). The Court did not review the lower court's construction of the competing assignment language. We therefore do not treat the decision as establishing that every occurrence of a particular verb formula guarantees an effective transfer. Earlier employee-invention doctrine also distinguishes obligations arising from being hired to invent from other employment relationships and from an employer's shop rights (Supreme Court of the United States, 1933, pp. 187\u8211?189). Historical scholarship documents changes in how employee-inventor interests were understood (Fisk, 1998).\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Copyright has a different structure. Initial ownership, work made for hire, and transfers are addressed in distinct statutory provisions. Ownership of a physical object is separate from ownership of the copyright embodied in it, and a transfer of copyright ownership generally requires a signed writing (United States Congress, 1976, sections 101, 201, 202 and 204). The agency inquiry used to determine employee status under work-made-for-hire doctrine cannot be replaced by a university's preferred label (Supreme Court of the United States, 1989). The position of academic and other creative employees raises additional questions about the relationship between employment and creative autonomy (Dreyfuss, 1987). We code what a policy reserves or claims, without resolving these external legal questions from its language alone.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 A broad definition also cannot create an intellectual property right that applicable law does not recognize. Facts are not protected by copyright, although an original selection or arrangement may qualify (Supreme Court of the United States, 1991). A statement that an institution owns research data could concern records, access, contractual obligations, a protected compilation, or several of these at once. It does not establish an exclusive copyright in every underlying fact. Similarly, the Copyright Office's analysis of artificial intelligence emphasizes human authorship and the nature of the human contribution (United States Copyright Office, 2025). That report is agency analysis, not a new ownership statute. Mentioning AI in a policy cannot settle copyrightability or eliminate the need to identify the relevant object.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 These distinctions generate a practical classification scheme. Each relationship has a rights-holder, a legal operation, a knowledge category, a trigger and a set of qualifications. The same provision may assign a university title while reserving a creator's noncommercial license. A broad resource-based presumption may coexist with an academic-work exception. Mixed and indeterminate cases are retained. The scheme is designed to test whether a change in definition reaches an operative rule, rather than assume that it does.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 4 Archive construction and research design\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The analysis begins with the original cleaned-sentence file from the Paper 1 replication repository. We pin its Git commit and SHA-256 checksum, together with the institution-year panel used only to distinguish direct observations from carried-forward records. The primary population remains 1944\u8211?2025. A single 1925 row is excluded; no other population repair is silently applied. The included file contains 87,159 cleaned sentences, 480 institution-year records and 518 institution-year-filename groups. The larger panel contains 4,277 rows, of which 3,797 are carried forward. These are not additional policy documents.\par

