\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs20 Notes: The primary population is unchanged from the established 1944\u8211?2025 sample. A filename group is an archival unit, not a verified unique policy or revision. Panel identifiers are used only for population reconciliation.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The audit assigns each sentence an identifier based on its immutable source row, rather than rely on sentence numbers being unique. It retains raw-text and normalized-text hashes and records institution, vintage label, filename and sentence number. Normalization is limited to case and whitespace for duplicate diagnostics. It does not modify the primary analysis population. Reconstructed context files preserve the available cleaned sentences, including collisions, but are explicitly labeled as reconstructions: missing headings, original paragraphs and external cross-references cannot be recovered merely by joining them.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Documentary structure is consequential. There are 29 institution-years with more than one filename. A university-year can combine a substantive policy with forms or contain documents for different campuses. The Wake Forest 2017 record, for example, includes both medical-center and Reynolda-campus files. Treating the pooled record as one unified legal instrument would obscure the population to which each rule applies. The audit also finds 3,662 rows involved in repeated sentence-number keys, concentrated in Georgia Tech, and 2,703 repeated normalized sentences within filename groups. Fifteen document groups participate in exact normalized-text duplication. Such repetition is retained in the primary corpus and examined in sensitivity analysis, rather than interpreted as independent legal evidence.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Historical coverage is highly uneven (Figure 1). The earliest decades contain very few observed institution-years and no observations occur in the 1950s. Absence from the archive does not establish absence of a policy. Nor does a vintage label prove when a provision became effective. One filename contains a year inconsistent with its upstream record, and a pair of Delaware vintages has the same normalized sentence set. The database therefore supports comparisons between observed textual endpoints, not a continuous census of policy revisions.\par

