\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 We first reproduce the original sentence screen without changing its expressions. A sentence is a candidate when it contains both a named knowledge category and one of the specified rule patterns. These include exceptions and resource conditions as well as ownership and assignment language. A candidate is consequently not a positive classification of university ownership. The screen yields 2,779 sentences in 449 records. The remaining records cannot be classified as lacking ownership rules: they may use terms omitted by the screen or distribute an operative rule across sentences.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 A second set of transparent expressions provides a broader review queue. It searches operations without requiring an asset term in the same sentence and records separate flags for nine knowledge categories, assignment wording, licenses, exceptions, procedures and triggers. Every output is marked as a machine-generated review aid and legally unadjudicated. We do not infer a rights-holder from the presence of an institution's name. The broader queue improves retrieval coverage for some inspected examples but has not been shown to improve precision or recall against a reference standard.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 For historical description, we compare adjacent observed vintages within each university. This yields 330 pairs from 129 universities. For each category, the measure is whether any included sentence contains its specified terminology. The transition matrix records absence or presence at each endpoint. It is a matrix of mentions, not ownership. A separate estimate averages the adjacent-pair change within each university and then gives each university equal weight. Percentile intervals resample those university means using 5,000 draws and a fixed seed. These intervals describe variation within the observed archive; they do not account for nonrandom archival selection, legal misclassification or date uncertainty.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 No annual interpolation enters these comparisons. The intervals between observed vintages vary, and their endpoints do not locate the timing of an intervening change. Thirty-five pairs change the number of constituent files. Even where file counts are unchanged, document authority or campus scope may differ. The within-university design reduces compositional differences across institutions but cannot solve these documentary problems. For that reason, we do not estimate a time trend in legal expansion or attach causal meaning to the lexical transitions.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 5 Results: language changes and legal relationships\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Table 2 reproduces the original screen by decade. Its rate changes markedly across the observed archive, but it is not a monotonic measure of institutional control. The denominator is the number of included cleaned sentences, which depends on document length, segmentation and coverage. Early estimates are based on very few institutions; later periods combine a broader collection of policy types. These pooled figures are useful for locating review work and diagnosing corpus composition. They are not estimates of the share of universities claiming ownership.\par

