\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Restricting comparisons by their archival structure also matters. Equal-university changes in data and software terminology remain positive when file counts are unchanged or both endpoints are from 1980 onward. Restricting gaps to at most ten years leaves 233 pairs from 97 universities; the software mean becomes -0.008, while the data mean is 0.050. The software pattern is therefore sensitive to which observed intervals enter the comparison. These restrictions do not authenticate policy scope, and shorter gaps select a different set of universities. The sensitivity outputs are retained in the replication package.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Screening results are not driven entirely by the identified sentence anomalies. Removing within-file repeated normalized sentences or excluding Georgia Tech changes the number of matches and the per-sentence rate but leaves the basic finding that the screen locates only a subset of potentially relevant text (Table 5). Restricting to observations from 1980 onward is another descriptive sensitivity check, not a test of the statute. These exercises establish robustness of a retrieval count to specific corpus choices. They do not validate the legal content of those matches.\par

