\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Accessible source context explains why the legal question cannot be answered by those matrices. Creighton's 2019 policy conditions its institutional claim on more than incidental university resources and expressly subjects it to exclusions. It separately provides a reciprocal nonexclusive license, protects specified academic works subject to work-for-hire and direct-funding qualifications, and includes an assignment form (Creighton University, 2019, sections C, D.2 and Appendix I). These are different legal operations in one document. Several are missed by the original screen because the operative sentence uses an umbrella term or refers to an adjacent list. A form's assignment language also does not show that a particular researcher executed it.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Illinois State's 2024 policy illustrates how categories cross allocation boundaries. Its academic-work provision includes educational software among creator-owned examples, while its patentable-research provision states a normal university ownership rule tied to employment and separately addresses substantial resource use. Its student-coursework provision protects work made with ordinary course resources, subject to additional arrangements for relevant sponsored projects (Illinois State University, 2024, sections 2.9, 2.12 and 3.1\u8211?3.2). Some numbered cross-references do not align with the displayed definitions, a further reason to retain interpretive uncertainty. Neither a software mention nor a student mention provides a complete ownership classification.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Wake Forest's 2017 Reynolda policy connects an introductory ownership and assignment statement to separate conditions for faculty, other employees, nonemployee students and other participants. Individual innovations outside the specified conditions remain with their innovators. Traditional scholarship is addressed through a separate copyright policy, which has not been authenticated here for that vintage (Wake Forest University, 2017, sections 3.1\u8211?3.4). The retrieved earlier patent policy already includes device-like software, but the copy bears a 2006 amendment date despite its 1999 adoption history (Wake Forest University, 2006). We can compare the accessible wording; we cannot certify a 1999-to-2017 legal transition or interpret the change as newly claiming software. The later pooled institution-year also includes a different campus's document.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The historical material presents a similar challenge. Palmer's account of Caltech reports a 1945 adoption history, describes assignment linked to duties or facilities, and preserves an own-time, no-facilities inventor category (Palmer, 1962, pp. 55\u8211?57). This corroborates the historical existence of a conditional allocation architecture. It is not an exact authentication of the cleaned 1945 sentences: the modal wording differs. Substituting the later reproduction for the missing original would erase precisely the distinction between a recommendation and an obligation that the research seeks to measure.\par

