\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs20 Notes: These are selected AI-assisted close readings and textual comparisons, not independently validated legal transitions. The evidence ledger supplies stable sentence IDs, sections, source URLs, hashes, qualifications and interpretation status. No frequency or direction of ownership expansion is inferred from this purposive selection.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The examples establish that conditional claims, retained rights and assignment mechanisms coexist in the accessible documents. They also demonstrate specific ways a sentence screen misses legally relevant relationships. They cannot establish how common those relationships are across the archive or how they changed over time. No institution-year has completed exhaustive contextual adjudication in this release. Consequently, the prevalence of institution-wide ownership presumptions, assignment obligations and creator or student exceptions remains unidentified; the unconstrained bounds on each binary population proportion are zero to one. A confirmed positive clause in a selected source does not resolve the completeness and scope of an institution-year bundle.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 6 Validation, competing explanations and limits\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Automated text analysis requires validation for the quantity being measured (Grimmer and Stewart, 2013; Gentzkow et al., 2019). Agreement between two keyword systems would not validate a legal interpretation, and agreement among repeated AI outputs would not constitute independent human annotation. We provide a stratified sample of 178 sentences, including both screen-positive and screen-negative cases. It covers decades and 68 institutions, with known inclusion probabilities. Category coverage is reported separately because rare categories are not guaranteed adequate representation. The annotation template remains unfilled pending a credible contextual reference standard.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The selected source readings are a different exercise. They were chosen to investigate apparent assignment, exception, license and historical-context problems, and their interpretations are explicitly AI-assisted. They show actual failures of the original retrieval logic: a defined term can replace an asset name, operative conditions can occur in the next sentence, and a retained license can matter without transferring title. They do not supply a probability sample of errors. We therefore report no precision, recall, kappa or category accuracy. Agreement statistics require appropriate annotation data and assumptions; they are not a substitute for correctness (Artstein and Poesio, 2008).\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 A complete validation would link each sampled provision to an authenticated version, have independent qualified readers identify its relationships and qualifications, retain disagreements, and adjudicate them transparently. Negative screen cases are essential to recall estimation. Rare categories require additional sampling with recorded probabilities, rather than an unweighted collection of convenient examples. Context-unverified provisions should remain unknown, including when a sentence appears linguistically unambiguous. The released codebook specifies these rules so that later validation can change labels without changing the frozen population.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Several explanations compete with ownership expansion. New terminology may clarify an old umbrella claim; a consolidated policy may add previously separate documents; a new exception may introduce the very category it protects. Longer policies create more opportunities for a lexical match. Changes in digitization or sentence cleaning may alter what survives in the corpus. System-wide policies repeated across campuses can give one legal instrument several apparent institutional observations. These mechanisms are consistent with some of the observed lexical changes and cannot be distinguished by their direction alone.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Within-university comparisons address one concern but leave others intact. They avoid interpreting a later sample of different universities as institutional change, yet still require comparable documents at both endpoints. A university may separate its medical and general-campus rules, replace a policy with guidance, or publish a form alongside a substantive instrument. The evidence required to call a transition is therefore more demanding than institution identity plus chronological ordering. A common actor and fact pattern must face a different rule under two authenticated versions. Where that comparison cannot be made, an unresolved transition is the accurate observation.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The source recovery undertaken here is necessarily partial. Accessible institutional sources were obtained for selected contemporary provisions, an earlier reprint and a historical survey. They are not a recovered archive of all underlying policies. The cleaned corpus lacks a complete source-URL manifest, some links fail, and official current pages cannot establish their earlier contents merely because they are authoritative today. We preserve successful retrieval hashes and failed attempts. Reconstructed sentence context is useful for review but is never presented as original paragraph context.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 Finally, neither this archive nor the selected policies establish enforceability. We do not observe all signed agreements, sponsored-research contracts, state-law restrictions or disputes. A university may announce a claim that would fail in a particular case, or possess a contractual right not fully expressed in a public policy. The article concerns stated institutional allocation and the evidence required to measure its evolution. It does not equate those statements with the legal rights ultimately recognized by a court.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b 7 Discussion and conclusion\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The evidence supports a restrained answer to the research question. The observed language changes within universities, and the accessible provisions express heterogeneous combinations of ownership, assignment, retained use and exceptions. Broader terminology does not by itself establish broader institutional ownership. The present archive cannot determine whether American universities generally expanded or contracted their claims over the period studied. That proposition remains open rather than confirmed by a proxy.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 For institutional economics, this matters because a nominal ownership indicator can conceal the decision rights that affect behavior. An institution that owns a work but grants its creator a durable research license differs from one that restricts continued use. An assignment form can alter the stated transfer mechanism without enlarging the category of covered works. An academic-work exception can preserve creative discretion within a broader administrative framework. Studying these arrangements requires keeping their constituent rights separate long enough to see how they interact.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 For empirical legal research, the archive demonstrates the importance of documentary comparability. Dates, campus scope and cross-references are part of the measurement problem, not ancillary bibliographic details. A later reprint can be valuable historical evidence without authenticating every word of an earlier policy. Repeated text can reveal common institutional rules while failing to supply independent observations. Those distinctions are especially important when a historical corpus was originally constructed for a different research question.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The replication materials make the positive findings and the unresolved work inspectable. They freeze the original population, reproduce the screening counts, generate historical lexical comparisons, document selected source readings and expose the absence of a legal reference standard. The next evidentiary step is contextual adjudication of comparable provisions, not additional regressions on unvalidated ownership proxies. Until that step is completed, the defensible contribution is a historical archive audit and a legally grounded account of why definitions and ownership must be measured separately.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs28\b Data, code and research transparency\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 The repository contains immutable source identifiers and checksums, construction and analysis scripts, deterministic sampling, derived results, a legal codebook, an annotation template and a source-linked evidence ledger. Raw text and downloaded policy documents are retrieved locally rather than newly redistributed in this package; third-party rights have not been comprehensively assessed. Each table and figure is linked to its generating output. The canonical manuscript is synchronized into a self-contained Overleaf export. Author identity, affiliations, funding and conflict declarations must be supplied and confirmed before submission.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa100\sl300\slmult1\ql\fs24 AI assistance was used in programming, source discovery, bibliographic comparison, close reading and drafting. The interpretations attributed to that process have not been independently validated by human legal coders. The authors must review and assume responsibility for the manuscript before submission. No human coding, legal review or endorsement is represented as completed by this statement.\par

